$$\\begin{cases} 6x - 9y = 14 \\\\ kx + 12y = -7 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$-8$"},{"key":"B","text":"$-4$"},{"key":"C","text":"$4$"},{"key":"D","text":"$8$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'A system of two linear equations has no solution when the lines are parallel but not coincident, meaning the ratios of corresponding coefficients are equal but the ratio of constants differs. Setting $\\frac{6}{k} = \\frac{-9}{12}$ gives $\\frac{6}{k} = -\\frac{3}{4}$, so $k = -8$. Checking the constant ratio: $\\frac{14}{-7} = -2$, which does not equal $-\\frac{3}{4}$, confirming no solution (not infinitely many). The value $-4$ comes from partial reasoning such as setting $\\frac{k}{6} = \\frac{-9}{12}$ with incorrect cross-multiplication. The value $4$ results from dropping the negative sign and solving $\\frac{6}{k} = \\frac{3}{4}$. The value $8$ comes from ignoring the sign entirely and computing $\\frac{6}{k} = \\frac{9}{12}$.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);










