If one student who volunteered for $4$ hours is removed from the data set, which of the following statistics will change?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Mean only"},{"key":"B","text":"Median only"},{"key":"C","text":"Both mean and median"},{"key":"D","text":"Neither mean nor median"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The original sum is $1(2) + 2(3) + 3(4) + 4(6) + 5(3) + 6(2) + 7(1) = 2 + 6 + 12 + 24 + 15 + 12 + 7 = 78$. The original mean is $\\frac{78}{21} \\approx 3.714$. With $21$ values, the median is the $11$th value; cumulative frequencies are $2, 5, 9, 15$, so the $11$th value falls in the $4$-hour group, giving a median of $4$. After removing one student who volunteered $4$ hours, the new sum is $74$ and there are $20$ values. The new mean is $\\frac{74}{20} = 3.7$, which differs from the original mean. For the new median with $20$ values, take the average of the $10$th and $11$th values. The cumulative frequencies become $2, 5, 9, 14$, so both the $10$th and $11$th values still fall in the $4$-hour group. The median remains $4$. Therefore only the mean changes.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);






























- Architect Diébédo Francis Kéré grew up in Gando, a village in Burkina Faso with no electricity or running water.
- He won a scholarship to study architecture in Berlin, Germany, in 1998.
- His first major project was a primary school in Gando, completed in 2001.
- The school used locally sourced laterite clay bricks and passive ventilation through raised roof panels.
- In 2022, Kéré became the first African architect to receive the Pritzker Architecture Prize.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"After studying architecture in Berlin on a scholarship, Diébédo Francis Kéré returned to his home village of Gando to design a primary school that was completed in 2001."},{"key":"B","text":"Diébédo Francis Kéré, who became the first African architect to win the Pritzker Prize in 2022, grew up in Gando, Burkina Faso, a village without electricity or running water."},{"key":"C","text":"Having grown up in Gando, a village without electricity, Kéré designed his first school there using locally sourced laterite clay bricks and passive ventilation through raised roof panels — techniques suited to a community with limited infrastructure."},{"key":"D","text":"Kéré's Gando primary school, completed in 2001, used laterite clay bricks and a ventilation system based on raised roof panels, earning him widespread recognition."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The goal is to show how Kéré''s background shaped his design approach. The choice beginning "Having grown up in Gando, a village without electricity" explicitly links his upbringing in a resource-limited village to his use of locally sourced materials and passive ventilation, drawing a direct connection between background and design choices. The choice beginning "After studying architecture in Berlin" mentions the school but does not connect his upbringing to the design decisions. The choice beginning "Diébédo Francis Kéré, who became the first African architect" juxtaposes his award with his origins but does not describe his design approach at all. The choice beginning "Kéré''s Gando primary school" describes the school''s materials and recognition but omits any connection to his personal background.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

Ecologist Priya Chandrasekaran''s 2018 study of pollinator decline in the Western Ghats found that habitat fragmentation was the strongest predictor of reduced bee diversity. By analyzing 143 forest patches ranging from 0.5 to 340 hectares, Chandrasekaran demonstrated that patches below 12 hectares supported fewer than half the bee species found in continuous forest, regardless of pesticide use levels in surrounding agricultural land.

Text 2:
Entomologist Marcus Vreeland''s research in Costa Rican cloud forests, published in 2021, suggests that pesticide drift from adjacent farmland can suppress pollinator populations even in large, contiguous forest tracts. Vreeland''s team recorded a 37 percent decline in solitary bee abundance within 800 meters of farms using neonicotinoid-based treatments, a pattern that persisted even in forest blocks exceeding 500 hectares.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 presents findings that complicate Text 1's conclusion about the relative importance of habitat size versus pesticide exposure."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 replicates Text 1's methodology in a different geographic region and reaches the same conclusion."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 provides evidence that directly contradicts Text 1's data on the number of bee species in small forest patches."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 extends Text 1's argument by showing that habitat fragmentation affects cloud forests as severely as it affects the Western Ghats."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 1 concludes that habitat fragmentation is the strongest predictor of bee decline "regardless of pesticide use levels," while Text 2 shows that pesticide drift suppresses pollinator populations even in large, contiguous forests — findings that complicate Text 1''s downplaying of pesticide effects relative to habitat size. The choice claiming Text 2 replicates the methodology and reaches the same conclusion is incorrect because the two studies use different methods and reach different emphases. The choice about directly contradicting species counts misidentifies the relationship; Text 2 does not dispute Text 1''s data on small patches but rather challenges its broader conclusion about the primacy of habitat size. The choice about extending the fragmentation argument is wrong because Text 2 focuses on pesticide drift, not fragmentation.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

Linguist Helena Vasquez has proposed that the rapid adoption of loanwords from colonial languages into indigenous Mesoamerican languages during the sixteenth century was not simply a consequence of political domination. Vasquez argues that indigenous speakers actively selected foreign terms that filled genuine lexical gaps — words for technologies, administrative concepts, and agricultural practices that had no preexisting equivalent. This selective borrowing, she contends, reflects agency rather than passive cultural erosion.

Text 2:
A 2022 corpus analysis by linguist Tomoko Nagai examined 1,400 Spanish loanwords in modern Nahuatl and found that roughly 40 percent had displaced native terms that were still in active use at the time of borrowing. Nagai''s data suggest that many loanwords did not fill lexical gaps but instead competed with and eventually replaced functioning indigenous vocabulary, a process Nagai attributes to the prestige asymmetry between colonial and indigenous languages.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"As largely consistent with her own framework, since the displacement of native terms still required active choices by indigenous speakers"},{"key":"B","text":"As methodologically flawed because corpus analysis cannot determine whether native terms were in active use at the time of borrowing"},{"key":"C","text":"As evidence that challenges a key premise of her argument but does not necessarily undermine her broader claim about speaker agency"},{"key":"D","text":"As confirming her hypothesis that colonial languages introduced concepts with no preexisting equivalents in Mesoamerican languages"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Text 2''s finding that 40 percent of loanwords displaced functioning native vocabulary directly challenges Vasquez''s premise that borrowing filled genuine lexical gaps. However, Vasquez''s broader argument is about speaker agency — that borrowing reflected active choice rather than passive erosion — and Text 2''s prestige-asymmetry explanation does not fully resolve whether speakers exercised agency in adopting prestige terms. Vasquez would most likely acknowledge the challenge to her lexical-gap premise while maintaining that her larger claim about agency could still hold. The choice claiming the findings are largely consistent overlooks that displacement of active native terms directly contradicts the lexical-gap premise, making the findings more than merely consistent. The choice about methodological flaws is unsupported; nothing in Text 1 suggests Vasquez would reject corpus analysis as a method. The choice about confirming the hypothesis directly contradicts Text 2''s central finding that many loanwords replaced existing terms.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


A 2020 study by a team at Simon Fraser University attempted to settle the debate by conducting 518 freezing trials under rigorously controlled conditions, equalizing container geometry, water volume, and ambient humidity. The researchers found no statistically significant difference in freezing times between initially hot and initially cold samples, leading them to conclude that previously reported instances of the Mpemba effect likely resulted from uncontrolled experimental variables rather than a genuine thermodynamic phenomenon.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It describes an experiment whose findings cast doubt on the reality of the phenomenon introduced in the first paragraph."},{"key":"B","text":"It proposes a new theoretical mechanism that reconciles the competing explanations presented in the first paragraph."},{"key":"C","text":"It provides historical context that explains why the phenomenon discussed in the first paragraph was initially overlooked."},{"key":"D","text":"It presents additional examples of the phenomenon introduced in the first paragraph to demonstrate its prevalence."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The second paragraph describes a controlled experiment that found no statistically significant evidence for the Mpemba effect and attributed prior reports to uncontrolled variables, thereby casting doubt on the phenomenon introduced in the first paragraph. The choice about proposing a new theoretical mechanism is incorrect because the study did not offer a new explanation for the effect — it questioned whether the effect exists at all. The choice about providing historical context misidentifies the paragraph''s function; the study is contemporary (2020) and does not address why the phenomenon was historically overlooked. The choice about presenting additional examples is wrong because the paragraph reports an experiment that failed to reproduce the effect, not additional instances demonstrating it.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);













- Tardigrades are microscopic animals (0.1–1.5 mm) found in diverse habitats worldwide.
- In a 2019 experiment, researchers exposed tardigrades to the vacuum of space for ten days aboard the International Space Station.
- Sixty-eight percent of the exposed tardigrades survived rehydration upon return to Earth.
- Tardigrades survive extreme conditions by entering a desiccated state called a "tun," in which metabolic activity drops to 0.01 percent of normal levels.
- A 2021 genomic study identified a protein unique to tardigrades, Dsup (damage suppressor), that shields DNA from radiation damage by forming a protective cloud around chromosomes.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Tardigrades, microscopic animals measuring just 0.1 to 1.5 millimeters, have been found to survive in a remarkable range of environments worldwide, including the vacuum of space."},{"key":"B","text":"In a 2019 experiment aboard the International Space Station, sixty-eight percent of tardigrades survived ten days of exposure to the vacuum of space, demonstrating the extraordinary resilience of these microscopic animals."},{"key":"C","text":"Tardigrades endure otherwise lethal conditions through at least two mechanisms: a desiccated \"tun\" state that reduces metabolic activity to 0.01 percent of normal levels, and a unique protein called Dsup that forms a protective cloud around chromosomes, shielding DNA from radiation damage."},{"key":"D","text":"Although tardigrades are only 0.1 to 1.5 millimeters long, they possess a protein called Dsup that protects their DNA, which helps explain why sixty-eight percent of them survived exposure to space in a 2019 experiment."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The goal is to explain the mechanisms by which tardigrades withstand lethal conditions for a scientifically knowledgeable audience. The choice beginning "Tardigrades endure otherwise lethal conditions through at least two mechanisms" directly addresses the "how" by identifying both the tun state with its specific metabolic reduction and the Dsup protein''s chromosomal shielding function — mechanistic detail suited to a knowledgeable reader. The choice beginning "Tardigrades, microscopic animals measuring just 0.1 to 1.5 millimeters" describes their range and survival broadly but provides no mechanistic explanation. The choice beginning "In a 2019 experiment aboard the International Space Station" reports experimental results that demonstrate resilience but does not explain the underlying biological mechanisms. The choice beginning "Although tardigrades are only 0.1 to 1.5 millimeters long" mentions Dsup and cites the space experiment but omits the tun state entirely and frames the information around the size contrast rather than providing a comprehensive mechanistic explanation.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-28","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

- The baobab tree (Adansonia digitata) is native to sub-Saharan Africa.
- Its trunk can store up to 120,000 liters of water.
- This water storage allows the tree to survive droughts lasting several months.
- Local communities have historically hollowed out baobab trunks to use as water reservoirs during dry seasons.
- The tree can live for over 1,500 years.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The baobab tree, native to sub-Saharan Africa, can live for over 1,500 years and store up to 120,000 liters of water in its trunk."},{"key":"B","text":"Because baobab trunks can hold up to 120,000 liters of water, local communities have long hollowed them out to serve as water reservoirs during dry seasons."},{"key":"C","text":"Native to sub-Saharan Africa, the baobab tree survives droughts lasting several months by storing water in its massive trunk."},{"key":"D","text":"The baobab tree, which can live for over 1,500 years, is one of the most remarkable species native to sub-Saharan Africa."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to emphasize practical importance to local communities. The choice beginning "Because baobab trunks can hold up to 120,000 liters" directly connects the tree''s water storage capacity to the human practice of using hollowed trunks as reservoirs, making the community use central. The choice beginning "The baobab tree, native to sub-Saharan Africa, can live for over 1,500 years" focuses on the tree''s biological traits without mentioning community use. The choice beginning "Native to sub-Saharan Africa, the baobab tree survives droughts" emphasizes the tree''s survival mechanism rather than its utility to people. The choice beginning "The baobab tree, which can live for over 1,500 years" highlights the tree''s longevity and geographic origin without any reference to community relevance.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-28"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-